  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:21:13.186Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nUnder the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines, every Filipino citizen residing outside the Philippines on income from sources within the Philippines, and every alien residing in the Philippines on income derived from sources within the Philippines are required to file an individual income tax return. However, individuals receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, where the tax has been correctly withheld, are exempt from filing an annual income tax return.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Section 51**: This section outlines the requirements for individuals to file income tax returns, specifying who is required to file and the exemptions applicable.\n- **NIRC Section 51-A**: This section provides for substituted filing of income tax returns by employees receiving purely compensation income.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51 of the NIRC mandates that individuals who are Filipino citizens, whether residing in the Philippines or abroad, and resident aliens must file income tax returns on their income. Conversely, Section 51-A allows for an exemption from filing for employees who receive purely compensation income from a single employer, provided that the employer has withheld the correct amount of tax.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, this means that most individuals earning income in the Philippines must file a tax return to report their earnings. However, employees who have their taxes withheld at source and do not have other sources of income are relieved from the burden of filing, simplifying compliance for a significant portion of the workforce. This exemption encourages compliance and reduces administrative burdens for both taxpayers and the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).",
